# Which program opens an attachment of a given media type, installed as
# /etc/mailcap.
#
# Read by Claws Mail when a MIME part is opened (double-click on the
# attachment, or "Open" in its menu).  mailcap_get_command_for_type() in
# src/common/utils.c tries ~/.mailcap first and this file second; with no
# entry for the type it falls through to the "Open with" prompt, prefilled
# with mime_open_command from clawsrc but asking every single time.  An entry
# here is what makes "Open" open something.
#
# Claws' reader is not a full mailcap(5) implementation, and this file is
# written to what it actually does: the type is matched as an exact string
# (no wildcards, no "application/*"), the command is everything up to the
# next ';', and the only flags it understands are "needsterminal" and
# "test=".  '%s' is replaced with the path of the saved part.
#
# The mimeapps.list in /usr/share/applications is the equivalent for the
# file manager and the desktop; the two are separate mechanisms, and a type
# that should open the same way from both places has to be named in both.
#
# When the user ticks "Remember" in the Open with prompt, Claws writes the
# choice to ~/.mailcap, which takes precedence over this file.

# PDF goes to the browser: WebKit is built with its PDF.js viewer, so luakit
# renders a PDF given its path, and nothing else on the image displays one.
# The quotes keep a saved path containing spaces as one argument.  Claws
# splits the command itself (execute_command_line), not through a shell.
# Both spellings, because the reader's match is exact: application/pdf is
# what a compliant sender uses, and application/x-pdf is what enough others
# still send that a rule for one alone would be a prompt on the other.
application/pdf; luakit '%s'
application/x-pdf; luakit '%s'
